1.3 Depth effect must be real

A true foreground-over-clock effect requires appropriate foreground image data.

Use paired wallpaper assets where possible:

- A complete background image.
- A separate transparent foreground PNG/WebP layer containing the foreground subject.
- Optional metadata defining the clock position, subject position, and scaling.

Use actual transparent foreground images with alpha channels.

Do not place the complete original photograph over the clock, because doing so would hide the clock and defeat the depth effect.

Do not use a rough rectangular cutout or an obvious hard-edged mask.

If foreground masks or segmentation maps are used, render the actual subject with naturally preserved edges.

Where supported by the existing environment, an appropriate local subject-segmentation system may be used to create depth layers from user images. Any such implementation must be genuine, sufficiently accurate, and efficient.

Do not introduce a large machine-learning dependency blindly.

Do not make a cloud service or API key mandatory just to display built-in wallpapers.

If an ordinary imported photograph cannot be separated into suitable layers reliably, explain this limitation and offer a functional alternative: import a normal wallpaper or use a compatible transparent foreground image.

Never fake the depth effect with a flattened image.

